{% doc %}
  Emits the carousel classes that the shared `carousel_overflow` / `carousel_edge_fade`
  settings decide.

  Two decisions, one file, because the theme used to spell them three different ways: a
  `carousel--visible` class on the host, an `.overflow-visible` utility dropped straight
  onto `.swiper`, and a `carousel--hidden` class that had no rule anywhere and only worked
  because `overflow: hidden` is Swiper's own default.

  @param {object} settings - Section/block settings holding carousel_overflow + carousel_edge_fade
  @param {string} [part] - Which element is being classed. Default 'carousel'.

  'carousel' is the carousel host element, and takes the overflow class.

  'viewport' is the wrapper around .swiper and takes the DESKTOP edge fade. It has to be its own element: the fade
  paints ::before/::after that bleed outward by --page-padding, so on a clipped .swiper they would never be seen.

  'viewport-mobile' is a second wrapper around that one, taking the MOBILE edge fade. Two elements rather than one
  because the two regimes have different drivers: while Swiper is alive FlexCarousel toggles is--active from its own
  events, and where `destroyBelow: 768` tears it down a native scroll row takes over, which only swipe-component can
  follow. Both drivers write the same three state classes, so they get an element each and each clears its OWN
  element's state when it stops driving (swipe-component above the breakpoint, FlexCarousel on `carousel:destroyed`;
  FlexCarousel never touches a wrapper upgraded with `is="swipe-component"`). Where Swiper survives below the
  breakpoint (destroyBelow conditional on enable_swipe_on_mobile), the desktop wrapper simply keeps painting there —
  that is why the mobile fade setting stays gated on enable_swipe_on_mobile. Upgrade this wrapper with
  `is="swipe-component"` and put `swipe__element` on the .swiper for the mobile half to work.

  @example
  Host: class="carousel carousel--grid {% raw %}{% render 'carousel-class', settings: section.settings %}{% endraw %}"
  Viewport wrapper: class="{% raw %}{% render 'carousel-class', settings: section.settings, part: 'viewport' %}{% endraw %}"
  Known and harmless: the emitted string carries a leading newline, so a host renders
  `class="carousel carousel--grid \ncarousel--visible"` and an unset viewport wrapper renders
  `class="\n"`. A doc block absorbs the newline after it, leaving the `{%-` below no text
  node to trim. Both parse as the identical class list. The one-line spelling that removes it
  loses to `yarn format`, and 48 other snippets here share the shape, so it is not worth the
  deviation — the callers that care pipe through `| strip_newlines`.
{% enddoc -%}
{%- liquid
  assign part = part | default: 'carousel'

  if part == 'viewport'
    if settings.carousel_edge_fade
      echo 'edge__shadows edge__shadows--desktop z-0'
    endif
  elsif part == 'viewport-mobile'
    if settings.carousel_edge_fade_mobile
      echo 'edge__shadows edge__shadows--mobile z-0'
    endif
  elsif settings.carousel_overflow == 'visible'
    echo 'carousel--visible'
  else
    echo 'carousel--hidden'
  endif
-%}
